<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lament · c. 1250 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Stabat Mater</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin hymn from the 1200s about Mary, the mother of Jesus, and her sorrow as she stood by the cross.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>attributed to Jacopone da Todi or Pope Innocent III</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (plainchant); set to music by many later composers</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Latin Church; later the Roman Catholic Church</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known; the earliest notated copy belonged to Dominican nuns in Bologna, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>13th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (medieval Latin hymn of the 13th century, its author not known for certain)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stabat-mater">Stabat Mater</a> is a 13th-century Christian hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. It portrays her suffering as a mother at the crucifixion of her son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Its title comes from its first line. The hymn has long been ascribed to the Franciscan friar Jacopone da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todi">Todi</a> (c. 1230–1306), and also to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>. The attribution to Jacopone has been strongly challenged by the discovery of the earliest notated copy, in a 13th-century gradual that belonged to the Dominican nuns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>.</p><p>By the end of the 14th century the hymn was well known. Georgius Stella wrote of its use in 1388, and around 1399 it was sung in Provence during processions lasting nine days. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> later suppressed it as a sequence of the Mass, together with hundreds of other sequences. Pope Benedict XIII restored it to the missal in 1727, for the Feast of the Seven Dolours of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Blessed Virgin Mary</a>. It is sung today in the liturgy on the memorial of Our Lady of Sorrows. An indulgence of 100 days was attached to each recitation.</p><p>The poem survives in several versions; one commonly printed is the text of an 1853 Roman Breviary. Edward Caswall made an English translation that is not literal but keeps the original's trochaic metre and rhymes.</p><p>Few medieval texts have been set to music so often. Composers from Josquin des Prez and Palestrina in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, through Vivaldi, Pergolesi (1736), Haydn (1767) and Rossini (1831–1841), to Dvořák (1876–1877), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a> (1897), Poulenc (1950) and Arvo Pärt (1985) have written settings, and a website devoted to them counts some 250. Most are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. Karol Szymanowski's setting of 1925–1926 is in Polish, and George Oldroyd's of 1922 is in Latin with an English translation for Anglican and Episcopalian use.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A meditation on the grief of Mary as she stands at the foot of the cross where her son is dying. The mood is sorrowful and devotional, dwelling on a mother's suffering.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Stabat mater dolorosa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The sorrowing mother stood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">iuxta Crucem lacrimosa,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>weeping beside the Cross,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dum pendebat Filius.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>while her Son was hanging.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Cuius animam gementem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whose groaning soul,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">contristatam et dolentem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>saddened and grieving,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">pertransivit gladius.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a sword pierced through.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">O quam tristis et afflicta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O how sad and afflicted</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fuit illa benedicta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>was that blessed one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Mater Unigeniti.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother of the Only-begotten.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quae maerebat et dolebat,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who mourned and grieved,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pia Mater cum videbat</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the loving Mother, as she saw</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Nati poenas incliti.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sufferings of her glorious Son.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quis est homo qui non fleret,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who is the man who would not weep</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Matrem Christi si videret</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if he saw the Mother of Christ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in tanto supplicio?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in such great torment?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quis non posset contristari,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who could not be saddened</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Christi Matrem contemplari</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to behold the Mother of Christ</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dolentem cum Filio?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>grieving with her Son?</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 60 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pro peccatis suae gentis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the sins of his people</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">vidit Iesum in tormentis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she saw Jesus in torments</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et flagellis subditum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and subjected to the scourges.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vidit suum dulcem natum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She saw her sweet son</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">moriendo desolatum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>forsaken in dying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dum emisit spiritum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as he gave up his spirit.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Eia Mater, fons amoris,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah, Mother, fount of love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">me sentire vim doloris</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make me feel the force of sorrow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fac, ut tecum lugeam.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that I may mourn with you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fac ut ardeat cor meum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cause my heart to burn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in amando Christum Deum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in loving Christ God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ut sibi complaceam.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that I may please him.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sancta mater, istud agas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy mother, do this:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">crucifixi fige plagas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>fix the wounds of the crucified one</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">cordi meo valide.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>firmly in my heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tui nati vulnerati,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of your wounded son,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tam dignati pro me pati,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who deigned so much to suffer for me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">poenas mecum divide.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>share the pains with me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fac me tecum pie flere,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Make me weep devoutly with you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">crucifixo condolere,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>suffer with the crucified one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">donec ego vixero.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as long as I shall live.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Iuxta crucem tecum stare,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To stand beside the cross with you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et me tibi sociare</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and to join myself to you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in planctu desidero.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in lamentation, I desire.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Virgo virginum praeclara,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Virgin, most glorious of virgins,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">mihi iam non sis amara:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>be not now bitter to me:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fac me tecum plangere.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make me lament with you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fac ut portem Christi mortem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Make me bear the death of Christ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">passionis fac consortem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make me a sharer of his passion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et plagas recolere.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and dwell again on his wounds.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fac me plagis vulnerari,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Make me be wounded with his wounds,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fac me cruce inebriari,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make me be made drunk with the cross</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et cruore Filii.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and with the blood of the Son.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Flammis ne urar succensus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lest I burn, set aflame by fire,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">per te Virgo, sim defensus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>through you, Virgin, may I be defended</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in die iudicii</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on the day of judgement.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Christe, cum sit hinc exire,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ, when it is time to go hence,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">da per matrem me venire</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>grant that through your mother I may come</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ad palmam victoriae.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to the palm of victory.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Quando corpus morietur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the body dies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fac ut animae donetur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>grant that to the soul be given</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Paradisi gloria.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the glory of Paradise.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous (13th century); as quoted in Wikipedia's article (Latin Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://la.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stabat_Mater_(prex)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>16.3: 'plagas recolere' is literally 'to go over again / call to mind again the wounds'.</li><li>17.2: 'cruce inebriari' is literally 'to be made drunk with the cross'.</li><li>19.3: the palm is the sign of victory (of the martyrs).</li><li>lines 9.2-9.3: the verb &quot;fac&quot; (make) stands at the start of 9.3 in the Latin; the English moves it up.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="qzOmPUu-F_M" data-title="Stabat Mater: Pergolesi's setting (1736), Nathalie Stutzmann and Orfeo 55"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Pergolesi's setting (1736), Nathalie Stutzmann and Orfeo 55</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Nathalie Stutzmann. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qzOmPUu-F_M" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Josquin des Prez)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Orlande de Lassus)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1585</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1590</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater, H.15 and H.387 (Marc-Antoine Charpentier)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1685–1690</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Antonio Vivaldi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1712</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Domenico Scarlatti)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1715</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Alessandro Scarlatti)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1723</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Giovanni Battista Pergolesi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1736</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Joseph Haydn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1767</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Luigi Boccherini)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1781, 1801</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater in G minor and in F minor (Franz Schubert)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1815, 1816</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stabat Mater (Gioacchino Rossini)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1831–1841</span><span class="sg-chain-h">m